// TEST-ONLY host interface for arg-VALUE-capture conformance (design/cadenza-platform.md §9). NOT a platform
// capability — its OWN package, physically separate from the platform `wit/world.wit`, so it can never
// co-mingle with or leak into a platform world a real reducer/genesis imports (the platform world vocabulary
// stays = real capabilities only; this is test infra, like the harness's Recording decorators + Entry
// variants). Compiled only for the arg-capture conformance guests.
//
// It exists to catch a SILENT arg-marshalling miscompile: `validate` / the emit byte-oracle / a run-fires
// gate all pass even if a host op receives the WRONG bytes, because none asserts the marshalled ARG VALUES.
// A guest calls `probe` with KNOWN values; the harness records what the host RECEIVED; a Cadenza checker
// asserts the received value byte-for-byte. It is sized to the mixed-width variant-payload marshal (the
// register-flatten join valtype vs the memory max-natural store width — the asymmetric coercion whose wrong
// width is otherwise unobservable). Extending it to another arg shape is a new op or variant case, additive.
package cadenza:test-arg-probe;

// The nullary case is `absent`, not `none`: a top-level `None` constructor would shadow the builtin
// `Option.None` module-wide in the Cadenza guest + checker (which use `Option`), so the case is named to
// avoid the clash. The name is arbitrary to the marshal — the point is the payload widths, not the label.
interface arg-probe {
  // A variant with MIXED-WIDTH scalar payloads — exercises the register-flatten path: the canonical join
  // valtype is i64 here (a case is 64-bit). A sign-mangled `big` or a `small` read from the wrong bits is
  // caught by asserting the received value.
  // The nullary case is `absent`, not `none`: its value-form head is the Cadenza constructor name (`Absent`),
  // and a Cadenza checker cannot name a constructor `None` (it shadows the builtin `Option.None` any checker-lib
  // guest uses), so `none` here would make the received-value un-assertable. `absent` -> `Absent` is clean.
  variant mixed {
    absent,
    small(u8),
    big(s64),
  }
  // A variant whose cases are ALL <=32-bit — exercises the memory path: the register join valtype would be
  // i32 (4 bytes) but the canonical memory payload area is MAX NATURAL = 2 bytes (u16). A wrong 4-byte store
  // overruns into the next list element, caught by asserting adjacent elements stay intact.
  variant narrow {
    absent,
    a(u8),
    b(u16),
  }
  // A record carrying a variant FIELD + a scalar — the register-flatten (record-arg marshal) path.
  record probe-record {
    v: mixed,
    tag: s64,
  }
  // `r` drives the record-field (register-flatten) path; `items` the list-element (memory) path. No return —
  // the capture of the RECEIVED arguments is the whole point.
  probe: func(r: probe-record, items: list<narrow>);
}

// TEST-ONLY world for arg-capture conformance guests — beside the platform reducer worlds, never one of them.
// A guest exports the reducer `guest` interface (so the harness spawns + drives it exactly like a
// reducer-world guest) and imports `arg-probe`. It mirrors the platform `reducer-world` shape: exporting
// `cadenza:platform/guest` carries the reducer envelope types (message/response/notification/step) it `use`s
// from the cadenza:platform package — resolved cross-package via the world-artifact `--dep wit/world.wit`,
// exactly as `reducer-world` gets them — so the world declares no separate `reducer` import.
world arg-probe-world {
  import arg-probe;
  export cadenza:platform/guest;
}
